'''Sahra of Galwa''' (c. 1675 – 1740) was regent of the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa|Kingdom of Galwa]] from 1709 until her death in 1740. The mother of [[Cali IV of Galwa|Cali IV]], she governed the kingdom during the minority of her son, who was an infant at the time of the [[Burawa Imperial Split|Imperial Split]] of 1709. Sahra established the administrative and political institutions of the western Burawi kingdom, including the [[New Laws of 1710]], which centralised royal authority and created the provincial system that governed Galwa for decades.

The daughter of [[Faarax of Galwa]], a senior member of the [[Kadim-Galwa]] branch of the Burawi royal family, Sahra assumed the regency when her father's incapacity prevented him from serving as regent for the infant Cali IV. She led the Galwa faction through the negotiations that divided the Burawi kingdom, established the court at [[Hargiesa]], and maintained neutrality during the [[First War of the Isthmus]]. Her regency saw the beginning of the [[Bindorama Civil War]] in 1735, which concluded after her death with the creation of the independent state of [[Asikyira]].

== Early life and family ==

Sahra was born around 1675 in [[Galwa]], the daughter of [[Faarax of Galwa]], a descendant of [[Nuur III]] (r. 1608–1641) and a senior member of the Galwa branch of the Burawi royal family. Her father held substantial influence in the western provinces of the unified Burawi kingdom.

Around 1695, Sahra married [[Aadan of Fayeha]], a lord from the Fayeha branch of the Burawi family, a cadet line associated with the [[Fayeha]] region on the [[Great Kaftian Lake]]. The marriage strengthened ties between the Galwa and Fayeha branches of the [[Kadim]] dynasty.

Sahra gave birth to three daughters, whose names are not recorded in surviving sources, before the birth of her son Cali around 1709. The birth of a male heir after three daughters, and at an age considered advanced for a first son, was regarded as favourable within the Galwa court.

== Assumption of the regency ==

By the early 18th century, Sahra's father, Faarax, had become incapacitated due to age. Sahra, who had assisted him in administrative matters, assumed effective responsibility for the Galwa faction's affairs. Following the death of [[Andrias III]] in 1709 and the ensuing succession crisis, the Galwa faction initially proposed that Faarax serve as regent for the infant Cali IV. His incapacity prevented this arrangement, and Sahra assumed the regency in his stead.

The succession crisis resulted from competing claims to the Burawi throne. The Galwa faction supported the infant Cali IV, while the Kaysoor faction supported the adult claimant [[Iacob V]]. Negotiations mediated by the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] produced a settlement that divided the kingdom. Under the agreement, Cali IV became king of the western portion with Sahra as regent, while Iacob V ruled the eastern portion as the [[Kingdom of Burawa]].

== Regency ==

{{main|Kingdom of Galwa (1709–1743)}}

=== Establishment of the court ===

Following the split, Sahra established the Galwa court at [[Hargiesa]], a city on the western coast. The location provided distance from the disputed border with the eastern kingdom and access to [[Aethiopean Ocean]] trade routes. The court was drawn from officials who had served the unified crown and remained loyal to the Galwa faction.

=== Administrative reforms ===

{{main|New Laws of 1710}}

In 1710, Sahra promulgated the [[New Laws of 1710]], a legal code that established the administrative framework of the kingdom. The laws created a two-tier system of fourteen provinces and 115 counties, each governed by administrators appointed by the court. The reforms centralised appointment power in the regent and reduced the feudal prerogatives of the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] aristocracy.

The New Laws remained the administrative foundation of the Kingdom of Galwa throughout its independent existence. The centralised system contrasted with the parliamentary governance that developed in the eastern Kingdom of Burawa after 1709.

=== Foreign policy ===

Sahra maintained neutrality during the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716), in which the eastern Kingdom of Burawa participated as part of an Illypnian coalition against Fosian colonial powers. The decision preserved Galwa's resources during its formative years but limited opportunities for colonial expansion.

Relations with the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] were shaped by the Church's role in the 1709 settlement. Although the Church had supported the Galwa claim during the succession crisis, it shifted to a neutral position following the split. The [[Cardinal of Burawa]], seated at [[Weli Markos]], served as intermediary between the two kingdoms. Sahra accepted this arrangement, and the Church retained its privileged position in Galwa, though relations between the regent and the hierarchy remained formal.

=== Bindorama ===

{{main|Bindorama Civil War}}

The colonial protectorate of [[Bindorama]], assigned to Galwa under the 1709 settlement, presented the most significant challenge to the regency. In the 1720s, the Galwa government established a legislative assembly in Bindorama that favoured Galwa-aligned elites over Burawi loyalist settlers and indigenous communities. The policies violated the autonomy guarantees of the 1709 treaty and provoked opposition from excluded groups.

Armed conflict began in 1735. The [[Bindorama Civil War]] continued until 1742, concluding after Sahra's death with the independence of [[Asikyira]].

== Death and succession ==

Sahra died in 1740 at approximately 65 years of age. Following her death, Cali IV, then approximately 31 years old, assumed direct rule as king. Sahra was interred in the royal crypt at Hargiesa.

== Legacy ==

Sahra's regency established the political institutions of the Kingdom of Galwa. The New Laws of 1710 created the provincial administration that governed the kingdom until its reunification with Burawa in the 19th century. Her centralised, absolutist governance contrasted with the parliamentary system that developed in the eastern kingdom.

The Bindorama Civil War, which began during her regency, resulted in the loss of Galwa's colonial protectorate. The conflict demonstrated the limits of the new kingdom's capacity to project power and influenced the domestic reforms of Cali IV, who created the [[Parliament of Lords (Galwa)|Parliament of Lords]] in 1743.

